# Line endings are LF in the working tree on every platform, not just in the
# repository. Git's default on Windows is to check out CRLF, and a dozen tests
# in this suite are guards that read PRODUCTION SOURCE and match a pattern
# against it — "this function is called on the way out", "this file's SQL
# touches only its own table". Those patterns are written with \n, so on a
# Windows checkout they miss, and the failure arrives as three unrelated-looking
# regex mismatches rather than as "your files have carriage returns in them".
# That is exactly how it arrived: green on macOS and Linux, red on all four
# Windows legs, in guards that were correct about the code they were checking.
#
# `text=auto` still normalises to LF in the repository, which was already true.
# `eol=lf` is the half that was missing: it fixes the WORKING TREE too.
#
# test/repo.test.mjs asserts at runtime that no tracked source file contains a
# carriage return, so a checkout that ignores this file fails once, loudly, and
# says why — rather than through whichever guard happens to notice first.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, so Git never inspects or rewrites them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.icns binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.dmg binary
*.exe binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4 binary
